    "updated_at": "2022-11-16T22:02:00Z",
    "body": "> The test compares styles for HTML elements with only the UA stylesheet against non-HTML elements (with the same names) with the expected CSS as author-origin styles. So for any property that is not in the test's CSS the expectation is that it's the property's initial value.\r\n\r\nGot it, ok.\r\n\r\n> OK, great. Is there a preference or rationale for current behavior for any of these?\r\n\r\nIn terms of rationale, I'd have to go [line by line](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/resources/html.css;l=892;drc=c27ecc58d66498dd1dc2cc4e1bdb4accdef3067f) and see what the history is.\r\n\r\nOne question generally: for `<option>` and `<optgroup>` styling, we are only thinking about standardizing styles in the `<select multiple>` case, correct? Are we also thinking about standardizing the non-`multiple` case for `<select>`? And should it be the same set of styles? ",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
